% ============================================================================
% phoneme-segmentation — breaking a word into its individual sounds, an
% ELEVENTH literacy sub-skill in the ADJ stdlib (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% The exact OPPOSITE direction from `phoneme-blending.adj`: `phoneme-blending.adj`
% recalls the WORD formed by blending three separate sounds together
% (sound_one, sound_two, sound_three, word). This library recalls the reverse
% — given a word, what three sounds does it break into? — grounding the
% cited page's own "Segmenting sounds in a syllable" skill as a pure lookup:
% `phoneme_segmentation(word, sound_one, sound_two, sound_three)`. Recall is
% a binding query:
%
%     ? phoneme_segmentation(feet, $S1, $S2, $S3)   % f, ee, t
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a FOUR-column table like
% `phoneme-blending.adj`, with the word and sounds swapped in role): each
% `row` lowers to a ground relation `phoneme_segmentation(word, sound_one,
% sound_two, sound_three)` carrying the Reading Rockets citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% three sounds WITH their source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on any word not the one the cited page walks through. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind the three sounds and recall the word they
% segment from:
%
%     ? phoneme_segmentation($Word, f, ee, t)   % feet
%
% The one segmentation this table carries, as a little truth table:
%
%     word   sound_one   sound_two   sound_three
%     ----   ---------   ---------   -----------
%     feet   f           ee          t
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "Phonological and
% Phonemic Awareness: In Practice" module — the SAME already-vetted page
% `syllable-count.adj`, `phoneme-substitution.adj`, `phoneme-deletion.adj`,
% `syllable-substitution.adj`, `syllable-deletion.adj`, `onset-rime.adj`,
% `phoneme-blending.adj`, and `phoneme-addition.adj` all already cite, a
% different section of it (its "Segmenting sounds in a syllable"
% demonstration) — WebFetch-verified THREE separate times for consistency
% before writing, all byte-identical. The exact span (one sentence, stating
% both the word and the three sounds it segments into):
%
%   "Now I will segment sounds in a word, and I will use the caps to show
%    each sound. Watch me. The word is 'feet', /f/ [slide a cap], /ee/
%    [slide a cap], /t/ [slide a cap], feet [sweep finger below the caps
%    blending the sounds]."
%
% `trust consensus` — the same tier this page's sibling sections already
% earned. Deliberately scoped to ONLY this ONE segmentation the cited page
% walks through step by step — honestly narrow (mirroring
% `phoneme-blending.adj`'s and `phoneme-addition.adj`'s precedent of one
% clean row) rather than inventing a segmentation the source does not
% demonstrate. A future slice can add a second segmentation to this table
% once a comparably clean citation is found. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table phoneme_segmentation {
    columns word, sound_one, sound_two, sound_three

    row (feet, f, ee, t)

    source "Now I will segment sounds in a word, and I will use the caps to show each sound. Watch me. The word is 'feet', /f/ [slide a cap], /ee/ [slide a cap], /t/ [slide a cap], feet [sweep finger below the caps blending the sounds]."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/reading-101/reading-101-learning-modules/course-modules/phonological-and-phonemic-awareness/practice"
    trust consensus
}
